# Fault.Bounds on a Vector of PLAIN STRUCT elements (error-model.md §11
# Increment 2 (A), case (c)). The element is a by-value non-resource struct,
# so the OUT-OF-BOUNDS read must take the `Fault.Bounds` handler and yield the
# fallback struct — exactly like the scalar/resource element cases.
#
# ⚠ HELD-OUT from the self-host lock-in (`self_host_runtime`): the self-host
# lowerer SCOPES OUT case (c). The faultable `gorget_array_safe_get` route is
# gated to scalar (a) + resource (b) element dsts only — `gorget_array_safe_get`
# is deliberately excluded from `eindex_raw_getter`'s aggregate copy-out
# (lir_lower.gg), so a faultable struct read would mis-store the element void*
# into a struct slot. The self-host therefore falls through to the PANICKING
# `gorget_array_get` for struct-vector bounds reads (it panics on OOB instead
# of catching — an interim gap, NOT a wrong value). Closing it needs a
# distinguishing typed marker for the faultable raw `safe_get` so the aggregate
# copy-out can fire on the in-bounds continuation, plus a `Vector[Struct].get()`
# regression fixture — out of Inc2 scope (see TODO). This fixture is wired only
# as a Rust-gg regression (`fault_catch_bounds_struct`) and is NOT snapshotted,
# so the diagnostic `self_host_runtime_diff` surfaces the gap without locking it
# into the passing set.
struct Point:
    int x
    int y

void main():
    Vector[Point] pts = [Point(1, 2), Point(3, 4)]
    # In-bounds → the element itself (no fault).
    Point ok = (pts[1]) catch Fault.Bounds: Point(-1, -1)
    print(f"{ok.x},{ok.y}")
    # Out-of-bounds → caught, yields the fallback struct.
    Point oob = (pts[9]) catch Fault.Bounds: Point(-1, -1)
    print(f"{oob.x},{oob.y}")
